\paragraph{Aggregation and Deployment.}
Aggregation rules influence predictability but not $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s direction (Figure~\ref{fig:aggregation}); at $K{=}5$ all four methods converge to 7/12, while at $K{=}7$ Bayesian calibration neutralizes it (0/12; Appendices~\ref{app:aggregation_k5},~\ref{app:aggregation_k7}). Robustness-first selection ($\min \eta_{\max}$) achieves lowest ASR in all conditions (Figure~\ref{fig:panel_design}); clean $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is stable under 4/6 attacks ($\rho{\geq}0.91$; Appendix~\ref{app:attacked_keff}).

\paragraph{Bayesian Non-Monotonic Behavior.}
Bayesian calibration's $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance is non-monotonic (6/12, 7/12, 0/12 at $K{=}3,5,7$): at large $K$, the posterior concentrates on 2--3 accurate judges, compressing $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ variation (Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation_k7}).

